{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01QpNCXUrqgmDajsxHWj3NA4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HFPE2T7gmTxPfzG8SR4ZXM","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/noss-to-wim/bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KA.md","limit":120,"offset":60},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1225,"cache_read_input_tokens":34694,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1225},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c637cc40-8152-404a-aa5d-36736366ea90","uuid":"a93716db-03a5-409b-90fc-778faa34abb6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HFPE2T7gmTxPfzG8SR4ZXM","type":"tool_result","content":"60\t- D. 17\n61\t\n62\t**A6.** Which CAT rating is required for a digital multimeter used on BEV HV circuits?\n63\t- A. CAT I — 600V\n64\t- B. CAT II — 600V\n65\t- C. CAT III — 1000V\n66\t- D. CAT IV — 300V\n67\t\n68\t**A7.** What is the typical acceptable cell voltage deviation in a BEV battery pack?\n69\t- A. +/- 0.005V\n70\t- B. +/- 0.05V\n71\t- C. +/- 0.5V\n72\t- D. +/- 5.0V\n73\t\n74\t**A8.** Which of the following is NOT a BEV scheduled maintenance item?\n75\t- A. Cabin air filter replacement\n76\t- B. Engine oil change\n77\t- C. Brake fluid replacement\n78\t- D. 12V auxiliary battery check\n79\t\n80\t**A9.** In the hierarchy of controls, which is the FIRST (most effective) level?\n81\t- A. PPE\n82\t- B. Administrative controls\n83\t- C. Elimination\n84\t- D. Engineering controls\n85\t\n86\t**A10.** Why are BEV-specific coolants non-conductive?\n87\t- A. To prevent engine corrosion\n88\t- B. To maintain HV insulation between coolant circuit and chassis\n89\t- C. To reduce cost\n90\t- D. To improve engine performance\n91\t\n92\t---\n93\t\n94\t## SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (40 marks)\n95\t\n96\tAnswer ALL questions. Provide clear and concise responses.\n97\t\n98\t**B1.** (8 marks) List FOUR (4) regulatory authorities and their respective legislation governing BEV maintenance activities in Malaysia. Present your answer in a table format.\n99\t\n100\t**B2.** (8 marks) Describe the procedure for inspecting insulated gloves before use. State FOUR (4) conditions that require immediate replacement.\n101\t\n102\t**B3.** (8 marks) Explain the Job Hazard Analysis (JHA) process. List the FIVE (5) steps involved.\n103\t\n104\t**B4.** (8 marks) Describe the function of the following ECUs found in a BEV:\n105\t- a) Battery Management System (BMS) ECU\n106\t- b) Motor Control Unit (MCU)\n107\t- c) Vehicle Control Unit (VCU)\n108\t- d) Thermal Management ECU\n109\t\n110\t**B5.** (8 marks) Compare BEV scheduled maintenance with ICE vehicle maintenance. List FOUR (4) maintenance items present in ICE vehicles but absent in BEVs, and FOUR (4) items unique to BEVs.\n111\t\n112\t---\n113\t\n114\t## SECTION C: Structured Questions (40 marks)\n115\t\n116\tAnswer ALL questions with detailed explanations.\n117\t\n118\t**C1.** (12 marks) A BEV arrives at your workshop for its first scheduled maintenance at 15,000 km.\n119\t\n120\ta) Describe the FIVE (5) types of hazards you would identify in a JHA for this service. (5 marks)\n121\t\n122\tb) Explain the workplace preparation procedure you would follow before commencing work. Include at least FIVE (5) key preparation steps. (7 marks)\n123\t\n124\t**C2.** (14 marks) You are performing a BEV health check on a 2024 model year vehicle.\n125\t\n126\ta) Describe the systematic BEV health check procedure from vehicle reception to report submission. Include at least SEVEN (7) major steps. (7 marks)\n127\t\n128\tb) During the diagnostic scan, you discover the following:\n129\t- Battery SOH: 94%\n130\t- Cell voltage deviation: Cell #47 reads 3.72V while all other cells read 3.85V\n131\t- HV insulation resistance: 850 ohm/V\n132\t- Fault code P0A1F stored (Battery Energy Control Module)\n133\t\n134\tInterpret each finding and state what action is required for each. (7 marks)\n135\t\n136\t**C3.** (14 marks) Describe the complete procedure for the following BEV maintenance activities:\n137\t\n138\ta) Cabin air filter replacement — include all steps from glove box access to completion. (4 marks)\n139\t\n140\tb) Fluid level checks — describe the procedure for checking brake fluid and HV coolant. Include safety considerations for each. (5 marks)\n141\t\n142\tc) Underbody inspection — describe what items to inspect when the vehicle is raised on a lift. Include the critical safety consideration regarding lift point placement. (5 marks)\n143\t\n144\t---\n145\t\n146\t## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n147\t\n148\t### Section A (20 marks — 2 marks each)\n149\tA1: B | A2: C | A3: D | A4: B | A5: D | A6: C | A7: B | A8: B | A9: C | A10: B\n150\t\n151\t### Section B\n152\t\n153\t**B1** (8 marks — 2 marks each authority/legislation pair):\n154\t1. JPJ — Road Transport Act 1987 (Act 333)\n155\t2. DOSH — OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n156\t3. Energy Commission — Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447) / Energy Commission Act 2001\n157\t4. DOE — Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n158\t\n159\t**B2** (8 marks):\n160\tProcedure (4 marks): Roll cuff towards fingers to trap air (1m). Squeeze gently to check for leaks (1m). Visually inspect entire surface (1m). Check markings are legible (1m).\n161\tReplacement conditions (4 marks — 1 each): puncture, crack/cut, discolouration, exposure of inner insulation layer.\n162\t\n163\t**B3** (8 marks):\n164\tJHA explanation (3 marks): systematic process to identify hazards, assess risks, and determine controls for each work step.\n165\tFive steps (5 marks — 1 each): (1) Identify task and break into steps, (2) Identify hazards at each step, (3) Assess risk level (likelihood x severity), (4) Determine control measures, (5) Document and communicate.\n166\t\n167\t**B4** (8 marks — 2 each):\n168\ta) BMS ECU: Monitors battery SOC, SOH, cell voltages, temperatures; manages charge/discharge.\n169\tb) MCU: Controls motor performance, inverter operation, regenerative braking.\n170\tc) VCU: Coordinates overall vehicle systems; central controller.\n171\td) Thermal Management ECU: Controls cooling system — pumps, valves, fan operation.\n172\t\n173\t**B5** (8 marks):\n174\tICE items absent in BEV (4 marks — 1 each): engine oil/filter, spark plugs, timing belt, transmission fluid.\n175\tBEV-unique items (4 marks — 1 each): HV battery coolant, HV insulation resistance check, battery SOH assessment, software update check.\n176\t\n177\t### Section C\n178\t\n179\t**C1** (12 marks):"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c637cc40-8152-404a-aa5d-36736366ea90","uuid":"bd37407c-890e-4f52-8aac-474afd804226","timestamp":"2026-04-17T09:32:32.138Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/noss-to-wim/bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KA.md","content":"- D. 17\n\n**A6.** Which CAT rating is required for a digital multimeter used on BEV HV circuits?\n- A. CAT I — 600V\n- B. CAT II — 600V\n- C. CAT III — 1000V\n- D. CAT IV — 300V\n\n**A7.** What is the typical acceptable cell voltage deviation in a BEV battery pack?\n- A. +/- 0.005V\n- B. +/- 0.05V\n- C. +/- 0.5V\n- D. +/- 5.0V\n\n**A8.** Which of the following is NOT a BEV scheduled maintenance item?\n- A. Cabin air filter replacement\n- B. Engine oil change\n- C. Brake fluid replacement\n- D. 12V auxiliary battery check\n\n**A9.** In the hierarchy of controls, which is the FIRST (most effective) level?\n- A. PPE\n- B. Administrative controls\n- C. Elimination\n- D. Engineering controls\n\n**A10.** Why are BEV-specific coolants non-conductive?\n- A. To prevent engine corrosion\n- B. To maintain HV insulation between coolant circuit and chassis\n- C. To reduce cost\n- D. To improve engine performance\n\n---\n\n## SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (40 marks)\n\nAnswer ALL questions. Provide clear and concise responses.\n\n**B1.** (8 marks) List FOUR (4) regulatory authorities and their respective legislation governing BEV maintenance activities in Malaysia. Present your answer in a table format.\n\n**B2.** (8 marks) Describe the procedure for inspecting insulated gloves before use. State FOUR (4) conditions that require immediate replacement.\n\n**B3.** (8 marks) Explain the Job Hazard Analysis (JHA) process. List the FIVE (5) steps involved.\n\n**B4.** (8 marks) Describe the function of the following ECUs found in a BEV:\n- a) Battery Management System (BMS) ECU\n- b) Motor Control Unit (MCU)\n- c) Vehicle Control Unit (VCU)\n- d) Thermal Management ECU\n\n**B5.** (8 marks) Compare BEV scheduled maintenance with ICE vehicle maintenance. List FOUR (4) maintenance items present in ICE vehicles but absent in BEVs, and FOUR (4) items unique to BEVs.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION C: Structured Questions (40 marks)\n\nAnswer ALL questions with detailed explanations.\n\n**C1.** (12 marks) A BEV arrives at your workshop for its first scheduled maintenance at 15,000 km.\n\na) Describe the FIVE (5) types of hazards you would identify in a JHA for this service. (5 marks)\n\nb) Explain the workplace preparation procedure you would follow before commencing work. Include at least FIVE (5) key preparation steps. (7 marks)\n\n**C2.** (14 marks) You are performing a BEV health check on a 2024 model year vehicle.\n\na) Describe the systematic BEV health check procedure from vehicle reception to report submission. Include at least SEVEN (7) major steps. (7 marks)\n\nb) During the diagnostic scan, you discover the following:\n- Battery SOH: 94%\n- Cell voltage deviation: Cell #47 reads 3.72V while all other cells read 3.85V\n- HV insulation resistance: 850 ohm/V\n- Fault code P0A1F stored (Battery Energy Control Module)\n\nInterpret each finding and state what action is required for each. (7 marks)\n\n**C3.** (14 marks) Describe the complete procedure for the following BEV maintenance activities:\n\na) Cabin air filter replacement — include all steps from glove box access to completion. (4 marks)\n\nb) Fluid level checks — describe the procedure for checking brake fluid and HV coolant. Include safety considerations for each. (5 marks)\n\nc) Underbody inspection — describe what items to inspect when the vehicle is raised on a lift. Include the critical safety consideration regarding lift point placement. (5 marks)\n\n---\n\n## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n\n### Section A (20 marks — 2 marks each)\nA1: B | A2: C | A3: D | A4: B | A5: D | A6: C | A7: B | A8: B | A9: C | A10: B\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1** (8 marks — 2 marks each authority/legislation pair):\n1. JPJ — Road Transport Act 1987 (Act 333)\n2. DOSH — OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n3. Energy Commission — Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447) / Energy Commission Act 2001\n4. DOE — Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n\n**B2** (8 marks):\nProcedure (4 marks): Roll cuff towards fingers to trap air (1m). Squeeze gently to check for leaks (1m). Visually inspect entire surface (1m). Check markings are legible (1m).\nReplacement conditions (4 marks — 1 each): puncture, crack/cut, discolouration, exposure of inner insulation layer.\n\n**B3** (8 marks):\nJHA explanation (3 marks): systematic process to identify hazards, assess risks, and determine controls for each work step.\nFive steps (5 marks — 1 each): (1) Identify task and break into steps, (2) Identify hazards at each step, (3) Assess risk level (likelihood x severity), (4) Determine control measures, (5) Document and communicate.\n\n**B4** (8 marks — 2 each):\na) BMS ECU: Monitors battery SOC, SOH, cell voltages, temperatures; manages charge/discharge.\nb) MCU: Controls motor performance, inverter operation, regenerative braking.\nc) VCU: Coordinates overall vehicle systems; central controller.\nd) Thermal Management ECU: Controls cooling system — pumps, valves, fan operation.\n\n**B5** (8 marks):\nICE items absent in BEV (4 marks — 1 each): engine oil/filter, spark plugs, timing belt, transmission fluid.\nBEV-unique items (4 marks — 1 each): HV battery coolant, HV insulation resistance check, battery SOH assessment, software update check.\n\n### Section C\n\n**C1** (12 marks):","numLines":120,"startLine":60,"totalLines":321}}}
